§ CSLv3 EN ↔ CSL TRANSLATION
I> reconciled: pasted (compression rules), specs/ (deletion+transformation rules, full example)
I> 2026-04-16: worked examples rewritten domain-neutral (game content removed)
R! used for: onboarding humans, interop w/ non-CSL tooling, doc gen

§ EN → CSL DELETION RULES (what to strip)
| EN pattern                     | CSLv3    | reason           |
|-------------------------------|----------|------------------|
| articles (the, a, an)         | ∅        | zero information |
| "it is", "there is/are"       | ∅        | existential implied |
| "should", "must", "needs to"  | W! R! M? | modal operators  |
| "in order to"                 | →        | flow operator    |
| "such that", "where"          | ⊢ or scope| constraint      |
| "for example"                 | # eg:    | annotation       |
| "however", "nevertheless"     | ∅ or ⚠   | hedging → delete |
| "basically", "essentially"    | ∅        | filler           |
| "it's worth noting that"      | ∅        | pure waste       |
| "respectively"                | positional| structural      |
| passive voice                 | active   | always           |
| "is stored as"                | :: type  | type annotation  |

§ EN → CSL TRANSFORMATION RULES
| EN pattern                    | CSLv3 pattern              |
|------------------------------|----------------------------|
| "A is a B"                    | A :: B                     |
| "A is a type of B"            | A :: B                     |
| "A must B"                    | W! A B                     |
| "A should B"                  | R! A B                     |
| "A may B"                     | M? A B                     |
| "A has property P"            | A.P                        |
| "A contains B"                | A { B } or A.B             |
| "If X then Y"                 | X → Y or X ⇒ Y             |
| "If X then Y else Z"          | X ? Y : Z                  |
| "X because Y"                 | X ∵ Y                      |
| "Therefore X"                 | ∴ X                        |
| "X causes Y"                  | X ~> Y                     |
| "X yields Y"                  | X → Y                      |
| "X, Y, and Z"                 | [X, Y, Z] or X+Y+Z         |
| "all X are Y"                 | ∀x∈X: x :: Y               |
| "there exists X..."           | ∃x: ...                    |
| "X of Y"                      | Y.X (right-head TP)        |
| "not X"                       | ¬X                         |
| "X and Y"                     | X ∧ Y or X+Y               |
| "X or Y"                      | X ∨ Y or X | Y             |
| "is equivalent to"            | ↔ or ≡                     |
| "maps to"                     | →                          |
| "pre-condition"               | ⌊ ⌋                        |
| "post-condition"              | ⌈ ⌉                        |
| "invariant"                   | t∞:                        |
| "X is between A and B"        | X :: [A..B]                |
| "X can be A, B, or C"         | X :: A | B | C             |
| "for each X in collection"    | ∀X ∈ col:                  |
| "X should never exceed Y"     | ⌈X ≤ Y⌉                    |
| "X triggers Y"                | X ~> Y or X → Y            |
| "X emits event Y"             | X ! Y                      |
| "when X then Y"               | X → Y                      |

§ CSL → EN RULES (expansion, for docs)
  inverse of above
  render for humans unfamiliar w/ CSL
  R! every glyph → its EN equivalent from §01 table

§ MIXED MODE (readable spec)
  allow EN sentences surrounded by CSL structure when:
    ◐ explaining rationale to mixed-audience
    ◐ onboarding docs
    ◐ external APIs
  W! core spec body always pure CSL

§ FULL EXAMPLE — scheduler priority calc (domain-neutral)

  EN (189 tokens):
  "The task scheduler priority calculation works as follows. When the
   scheduler evaluates a pending task, it first retrieves the task's
   base priority and the waiting-time-so-far. It then calculates the
   aged priority by multiplying base priority by an aging factor, which
   is the waiting time divided by ten plus one. Next, the scheduler
   checks the task's deadline and subtracts the remaining time from
   the aged priority to get the deadline-adjusted priority. If the
   adjusted priority is less than one, it is set to one to ensure every
   task gets at least one cycle. Finally, a starvation boost is applied:
   if the task has been waiting longer than a threshold, the priority
   is multiplied by a starvation multiplier, which defaults to two.
   The final priority value is then used for scheduling."

  CSLv3 (42 tokens):
    § sched.prio.calc
      aged    = task.base * (task.wait / 10 + 1)
      adj     = max(1, aged - task.deadline.remaining)
      starved = task.wait > threshold
      final   = starved ? adj * (task.starve-mult | 2) : adj

  compression: 189 → 42 = 4.5×

§ SECOND EXAMPLE (ordering rule, neutral)

  EN: "The request pipeline should batch database writes by table,
       because connection churn is expensive. This must happen before
       the response-assembly step."

  CSLv3:
    R! req.pipe batch.writes ← table
    ∵ db.conn.churn = expensive
    W! order: batch ≺ resp.assemble

  compression: 32 → 12 = 2.7×

§ WHEN TO USE WHICH MODE
  ✓ pure CSL: specs, GDDs, architecture docs, agent CoT, CSLv3 compiler source
  ✓ pure CSL: handoffs between Claude instances
  ◐ mixed: code comments (short CSL ; EN if team-external)
  ◐ mixed: onboarding materials
  ✗ pure EN: user-facing chat when user writes EN (match register)
∎
